miadi-tide — the tide review loop and runtime on a Miadi host

Client
  tan <tmux-target>           review the last reply of the agent in that pane;
                              the review is typed back into the pane when you
                              submit. TANS=0 tan <target> opens it without
                              delivering. Other options: plannotator-tmux-review --help
  plannotator-tmux-review     the loop; /usr/lib/miadi-tide/plannotator-tmux-review.sh
                              from Miadi packages/tide/scripts (no Gaia checkout)
                              with its pane-write guard beside it (it will not type
                              into a pane whose human is mid-sentence, or whose
                              agent is mid-turn). The Miadi app's /tide cockpit runs
                              this copy too (@miadi/tide >= 0.6.6), unless
                              PLANNOTATOR_TMUX_REVIEW_SCRIPT names another
  plannotator-tui             the annotator (terminal UI and --blocks/--export/--annotate)
  tide                        the tide runtime CLI (tide --help)
  /usr/share/miadi-tide/node/@miadi/tide, @miadi/tide-contract
                              the Node client of the runtime, as it would be published

Server
  tide-runtime.service        the context daemon (tide daemon --interval 60), one per
                              user, off until that user enables it:
      systemctl --user enable --now tide-runtime.service tide-store-prune.timer
  tide-store-prune.timer      keeps the daemon's store (~/.miadi/navigator/context/)
                              to 7 days; MIADI_TIDE_KEEP_DAYS changes it
  A user's own ~/.config/systemd/user/tide-runtime.service (for example one
  written by `tide service install`) replaces the packaged unit.

The runtime venv
  /usr/lib/miadi-tide/tide-runtime is built at install from the wheels in
  /usr/share/miadi-tide/wheels, with --no-index and the pinned constraints.txt
  there. It uses the first Python 3.11+ with venv it finds (python3.13, 3.12,
  3.11, python3). If it goes missing: sudo dpkg-reconfigure miadi-tide

What tide needs to bring an agent back
  tide records no session itself. After tmux restores its panes (tmux-resurrect,
  `miadi-terminal enable restore`), `tide agents restore` relaunches the agents tide's
  last snapshot saw in them, from what other tools wrote. Which agents it sees:

  Claude Code  ~/.claude/sessions/<pid>.json, written by Claude Code itself, for the
               pane's process or its nearest descendant.
  Any agent    the last line for the pane in <sessiondata>/data/terminal_bindings.jsonl
               since the tmux server started: one line per session start, end and
               rename, written by capture hooks. The agent is running when the line's
               pid still runs its program, exited after a session.end line, else lost.
               This is the only way to see a Claude that exited before the save, and
               how Pi and Hermes are seen.
  Pi, Hermes   a command line in the pane that names its session, `pi --session <id>`
               or `hermes --resume <id>`, is seen without any hook.

  <sessiondata> is the first of CLAUDE_SESSIONDATA_ROOT, MIADI_SESSION_DIR,
  MIADI_SESSIONDATA_ROOT and SESSION_DATA_ROOT that is set, else /src/_sessiondata.

  Where the capture hooks come from:
    Claude   the miadi-session-observability plugin of this kit:
             claude plugin install miadi-session-observability@miadi-orchestration-kit
    Pi       pi_hooks/event_capture.ts, listed under `extensions` in
             ~/.pi/agent/settings.json
    Hermes   hermes_hooks/session_start_hook.sh and session_end_hook.sh, listed under
             `hooks:` in ~/.hermes/config.yaml
  The Pi and Hermes hooks are in jgwill/binscripts, a private repository. No package
  installs them. A Pi package is planned (jgwill/miadi-orchestration-kit#99).

  What the restore does with each agent:
    running   relaunched, a few seconds apart, oldest activity first, with `--resume
              <id>`. The agents beyond the memory left (16 GiB kept free) get their
              command typed without Enter.
    exited or lost
              nothing is started or typed. The pane keeps a note, `tide agents list`
              shows it, and `tide agents resume` in that pane brings the agent back
              when you choose.

  How each kind is resumed:
    claude   its launch alias, with `--resume <id>`. The alias is MIADI_LAUNCH_ALIAS
             when the launcher exported it (the aliases in binscripts do). Otherwise
             tide infers one from your interactive aliases, then falls back to the
             command line.
    pi       pi --session <id>
    hermes   hermes --resume <id>
    others   no resume rule, so a restore skips them (Codex is one).
  A Pi or Hermes pane without its capture hooks, and not started with its session on
  the command line, comes back as an empty shell.

  Check before a reboot: `tide agents list` shows each pane's agent, its state and
  session id. A pane missing from it will not be relaunched, and a pane marked exited
  or lost will not start by itself.
  The kinds are RESUME_RULES in tide_runtime/launchers.py: a kind added there needs a
  line here.

Replies and delivered reviews are kept in $MIADI_TIDE_DIR/reviews/<user>/
(PLANNOTATOR_TMUX_REVIEW_DIR), marks in $MIADI_TIDE_DIR/annotations/<user>/
(PLANNOTATOR_DATA_DIR). MIADI_TIDE_DIR is $MIADI_DATA_DIR/tide (/srv/miadi/tide)
unless miadi.env says otherwise; miadi-config's env.sh sets both in login
shells, and a service sets them in its own unit. Without miadi-config they fall
back to ~/.local/state/plannotator-tmux-review/ and ~/.plannotator/.

/usr/share/miadi-tide/SOURCE names the commit each part was built from.
https://github.com/jgwill/miadi-orchestration-kit/issues/55
